    recording: four provider artifacts shared one version property, and the ASM
    companion has NO release at the target version - its older line stops
    several patches short and then jumps to a much newer series - so the family
    was split into two version axes. Advancing one shared property would have
    failed resolution outright.
    Structurally the persistence layer is also lower risk than it looks,
    because the provider's dynamic weaving is DISABLED in the data-source
    configuration; weaving is where a provider manipulates entity bytecode at
    load time and is the layer's largest bytecode-related risk on a new class
    file version. That is a pre-existing property of the configuration, not
    something the migration introduces.
